			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1360324">
									

<p>I like this one. There are minor technical problems. Carbide lamps don't use bulbs, circuits, or batteries. According to Wikipedia,</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Carbide lamps, or acetylene gas lamps, are simple lamps that produce and burn acetylene (C2H2) which is created by the reaction of calcium carbide (CaC2) with water.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Simply stating that the lamp has been modified into an electrical light would fix this. Describing it as made in the style of an old carbide lamp would also work.</p>
<p>Bronze and brass are two distinctly different alloys. The Late 19th and Early 20th century used brass almost exclusively over bronze. A lamp made of bronze is certainly possible, especially if homemade.</p>

